WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.247
USB drives and removable media matter because they are simple, portable, and easy to overlook.

2
00:00:06.247 --> 00:00:13.353
A small device may hold documents, archives, scripts, executables, images, logs, backups, or staged data.

3
00:00:13.353 --> 00:00:27.331
External disks, SD cards, phones exposed as storage, and other portable media can appear in incident response, insider investigations, fraud reviews, policy investigations, and ordinary business workflows.

4
00:00:27.331 --> 00:00:32.017
Removable media can support many different investigative questions.

5
00:00:32.017 --> 00:00:42.793
It may show legitimate business use, authorized file transfer, temporary staging, software delivery, backup activity, data movement, or unauthorized copying.

6
00:00:42.793 --> 00:00:53.101
It may also be relevant when malware appears to have arrived through a portable device or when sensitive files are found outside expected storage locations.

7
00:00:53.101 --> 00:00:57.552
The same artifact can have different meaning depending on context.

8
00:00:57.552 --> 00:01:00.129
Careful interpretation is essential.

9
00:01:00.129 --> 00:01:03.096
Device presence is not the same as device use.

10
00:01:03.096 --> 00:01:05.751
Device use is not the same as file access.

11
00:01:05.751 --> 00:01:08.406
File access is not always file transfer.

12
00:01:08.406 --> 00:01:11.998
File transfer is not automatically malicious intent.

13
00:01:11.998 --> 00:01:23.243
A USB connection record, a matching volume label, or a copied file timestamp may be important evidence, but each must be tested against the broader timeline and case facts.

14
00:01:23.243 --> 00:01:28.319
Authorized, documented, evidence-preserving practice is the foundation.

15
00:01:28.319 --> 00:01:42.375
Investigators should know the authority for the work, preserve the original media where possible, maintain chain of custody, document tools and methods, and avoid unnecessary interaction with unknown devices.

16
00:01:42.375 --> 00:01:45.499
The goal is not to make the evidence fit a theory.

17
00:01:45.499 --> 00:01:49.950
The goal is to preserve and interpret artifacts in a defensible way.

18
00:01:49.950 --> 00:02:01.117
Removable media analysis is strongest when it combines host artifacts, media contents, file system metadata, timeline correlation, user context, and policy context.

19
00:02:01.117 --> 00:02:12.205
The investigator's job is to explain what the evidence supports, what remains uncertain, and what additional evidence could corroborate or challenge the conclusion.
